    {
      "id": "xEk4HIMc5c6YNkJjYmVru",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 9351.536867592362,
      "y": 5699.067757624243,
      "width": 1784.0877685546875,
      "height": 2565,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#f08c00",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bCp",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 508811028,
      "version": 3527,
      "versionNonce": 1939814839,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1752469072139,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. frequent creation and deletion\n   of kafka topic for doc is not correct.\n   and if doc is not editing much\n   what about that topic and its partition ? \n2. if we don't do specific topic for doc\n    neighbouring doc issue , which is not good\n    ux, if its getting delayed.\n3. but server can we scaled horizontally\n    with consistency hashing.\n4. so first on ui it will update optimistically.\n    and in worst case ( server fail to push to kafka) \n    if it fail it will revert that particular operation on UI.\n5. here the topic can ben general, its ok other doc\n    operation affecting it. its offline.\n6. else its a grpc connection very efficient.\n7. server with consistency hahsing maintain the local copy of\n   doc since consistency hashing, it will receive operation of \n   particular doc, it will assign id and pushblish to kafka \n   orderwise.\n8. once done, it will apply operation locally once done, it will \n    broadcast to other user of that doc, usually very less \n   like peak 15. its like group of 15 member in chat. \n9. websocket receive it if user online apply .\n10. on client side its also maintain its local OT and save there\n    resolve the operation and apply.\n11. on explicitly save server will save doc to db.\n12. else it will periodically save it. and also periodically remove inmemory\n    doc if someone is not online.\n11. what if server fail ? -> new one pull the doc and start, at first request\n    of operation.\n12. what if fail after kafka push. hmm interesting. \n     - On both UI it they will se their operation but not interchange for some time.\n     - we can maintain the doc:offset -> which is done. may be in redis \n     - after server come, it will replay the message from that offset upto the last one.\n     - kafka will act as event sourcing. \n     - heck -> can write code in that way it will know current its processing the kafka pending events.\n             -> if will check every time its new operation came ? \n             -> if yes it will pick from inmemory than kafka.\n\n    - here its tradeoff -> consistency vs availability. - > can chhose one.\n                            -> either UI stop until server replay pending events. \n                            -> or UI allow but might endup inconsistent state for some time.\n\n\n    - flow -> u1 -> \"a\". , u2 -> \"b\"   -> both push to kafka ( u1, u2. ) now server faied.\n    - new server got request. u1 -> \"ap\" then u2 -> \"bq\" ( u1, u2 ). code parallely.\n      keeping this message in kafka and inmemory.  \n    - new server first pull the doc from db and offset from redis.\n    - two cases -\n                - if some operation already updated in db. but not on ui -> send.\n                - if doc is not complete , pull event from kafka with redis offset\n                   to first new message -> and send to UI.\n                - after that start reading from inemmroy ops.\n                - now its stable as previous. \n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. frequent creation and deletion\n   of kafka topic for doc is not correct.\n   and if doc is not editing much\n   what about that topic and its partition ? \n2. if we don't do specific topic for doc\n    neighbouring doc issue , which is not good\n    ux, if its getting delayed.\n3. but server can we scaled horizontally\n    with consistency hashing.\n4. so first on ui it will update optimistically.\n    and in worst case ( server fail to push to kafka) \n    if it fail it will revert that particular operation on UI.\n5. here the topic can ben general, its ok other doc\n    operation affecting it. its offline.\n6. else its a grpc connection very efficient.\n7. server with consistency hahsing maintain the local copy of\n   doc since consistency hashing, it will receive operation of \n   particular doc, it will assign id and pushblish to kafka \n   orderwise.\n8. once done, it will apply operation locally once done, it will \n    broadcast to other user of that doc, usually very less \n   like peak 15. its like group of 15 member in chat. \n9. websocket receive it if user online apply .\n10. on client side its also maintain its local OT and save there\n    resolve the operation and apply.\n11. on explicitly save server will save doc to db.\n12. else it will periodically save it. and also periodically remove inmemory\n    doc if someone is not online.\n11. what if server fail ? -> new one pull the doc and start, at first request\n    of operation.\n12. what if fail after kafka push. hmm interesting. \n     - On both UI it they will se their operation but not interchange for some time.\n     - we can maintain the doc:offset -> which is done. may be in redis \n     - after server come, it will replay the message from that offset upto the last one.\n     - kafka will act as event sourcing. \n     - heck -> can write code in that way it will know current its processing the kafka pending events.\n             -> if will check every time its new operation came ? \n             -> if yes it will pick from inmemory than kafka.\n\n    - here its tradeoff -> consistency vs availability. - > can chhose one.\n                            -> either UI stop until server replay pending events. \n                            -> or UI allow but might endup inconsistent state for some time.\n\n\n    - flow -> u1 -> \"a\". , u2 -> \"b\"   -> both push to kafka ( u1, u2. ) now server faied.\n    - new server got request. u1 -> \"ap\" then u2 -> \"bq\" ( u1, u2 ). code parallely.\n      keeping this message in kafka and inmemory.  \n    - new server first pull the doc from db and offset from redis.\n    - two cases -\n                - if some operation already updated in db. but not on ui -> send.\n                - if doc is not complete , pull event from kafka with redis offset\n                   to first new message -> and send to UI.\n                - after that start reading from inemmroy ops.\n                - now its stable as previous. \n\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
